# Engagement Targets

> Who you comment on, and why. Fill this during/after `/onboard`. Three groups work well —
> adapt to your strategy. Add LinkedIn URLs and the outcome you want from each.

## Geography

Which markets you source from, and the exception that lets a non-primary-market account in.
Per-group markets live in `references/engagement-profile-criteria.md`; this is the summary.

- Primary market: [ xxx ]
- The audience exception: a voice outside your market enters only when their audience IS your
  buyer. Whose audience it is, not how large.

---

## Hard exclusions (never engage, regardless of fit, reach or content quality)

> **`/profile-check` reads this section and stops on a hit.** These are automatic. They are not
> weighed against ICP fit or post quality, and a good post does not override them. Checked BEFORE
> any classification work.
>
> Leave this list empty if nothing applies to you. An empty list is a valid answer, and the skills
> handle it. Do not invent exclusions to fill it.

**1. Direct competitors.** [ xxx — the test that decides it. The default is all four of same
buyer, same problem, same offer, same market; three or fewer is an adjacent business and a
possible referral partner, not an exclusion. See `references/engagement-profile-criteria.md`. ]

**2. Regulated-field exclusions, if your field is regulated.** [ xxx — delete this whole entry if
you hold no licence and work in no regulated field. Where it applies, name the accounts you may
not comment under, and note that the exclusion follows the VENTURE, not the person: someone can
run an excluded business and a clean one. A venture-level admission does not survive a headline
that advertises the excluded business. ]

**3. [ xxx — any further category you decide on. ]**

**Post-type filters.** An account can be admitted with a filter rather than blocked outright,
where only part of their content is unsafe. Write the filter into that account's row as
*"comment ONLY on X, SKIP Y"*, and `/commenting-others` applies it before drafting.

